WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:02.670
Representatives from around 200 nations have
begun crunch UN climate talks in Poland.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:03.670
The summit comes against a backdrop of dire
environmental warnings and a call for action

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:04.670
against the threats posed by climate change.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:05.670
Delegates will seek ways of implementing the
commitments made in the 2015 Paris treaty

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.670
to limit the rise in global temperatures.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.670
This is where delegates from nearly 200 countries
will be scrambling over the next two weeks

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.170
to reach a deal that can save the planet.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.440
The meeting in Katowice - at the heart of
Poland's coal region -- comes three years

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.910
after the landmark Paris accord.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:21.420
The envoys gathering at COP 24 aim to produce
a rule book to flesh out the broad details

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:26.490
that were agreed in Paris -- limiting the
rise in global temperatures to between 1 point

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.660
5 and 2 degrees celcius.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.730
The sense of urgency is palpable.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:40.809
This is a very, very important conference,
it takes place also in a scenario where we

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:47.129
have clear signals about the urgency with
which we need to address the issues of climate

00:00:47.129 --> 00:00:48.129
change.

00:00:48.129 --> 00:00:56.359
The effects that are happening are affecting
communities around the world, casualties,

00:00:56.359 --> 00:01:02.219
destruction in so many places.

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:07.689
In Brussels, tens of thousands of people demonstrated
to raise awareness of climate change.

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:12.119
And the protesters had a message for the delegates
gathering in Poland.

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:20.359
There's obviously in the next like ten years,
we have very much a window of opportunity

00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:21.359
that we need to really grasp with both hands.

00:01:21.359 --> 00:01:22.359
It's now or never
I think if we start a plan of action now,

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:29.850
then like our grandchildren might still have
a chance to have a planet, so we just need

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.494
to start now and just stop losing time."

00:01:31.494 --> 00:01:33.329
And time is of the essence.

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:38.700
To keep global warming well under 2 degrees,
experts say emissions must drop by up to 70

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.829
percent over the next three decades.

00:01:40.829 --> 00:01:50.659
But global emissions rose in 2017, and are
on track to do the same this year.

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:56.249
The venue for the UN climate talks in Katowice
- it's shrouded in smog from a power plant

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:58.439
fuelled by coal.

00:01:58.439 --> 00:02:02.310
The polluting hydrocarbon has been mined here
since the 18th century.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.359
Around 100,000 people in the region work in
the coal industry.

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:09.319
Coal is a treasure!

00:02:09.319 --> 00:02:11.620
If there was no coal, there would be no work.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:18.900
If the mines closed, all the shops and businesses
in the region would go bankrupt.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.959
Poland will continue to generate most of its
electricty from coal until 2050, meaning that

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:29.590
the mines of Katowice will not shut down any
time soon.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.400
Coal is what sustains Upper Silesia.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:36.510
It is the primary product of this region,
and of course many people here are biased

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:37.510
towards it.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:41.620
But we know which way the wind is blowing
all over the world, and which path we should

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:48.470
be going down.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:53.069
Only 15 percent of electricity produced in
Poland is from renewable sources.

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:56.860
Activists complain that the government has
neglected to develop the alternative energy

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.160
sector.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:05.740
Activists, scientists, citizens, the mining
and power generation sector have all said:

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.549
We are ready for change.

00:03:07.549 --> 00:03:17.599
The only ones who are not interested are politicians.

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:21.180
For Poland's government Katowice is a model
green city.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:24.180
It boasts a research centre for renewable
energy.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.650
A shopping mall has been built on top a closed
down coal mine.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:33.709
Even the conference centre that hosts the
climate talks stands on the site of a former

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:34.709
mine.

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:52.950
But the fact remains: Katowice and coal are
inextricably linked, and will be for

00:03:52.950 --> 00:05:38.440
some time

00:05:38.440 --> 00:06:33.590
to come.

